      "problem": "Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe integer $14$ \n{{field-1}}\nthe additive inverse of $13$ modulo $37$ since $14+13$ {{field-2}} $(\\text{mod}\\,37).$",
      "worked_solution": "The additive inverse of $13$ modulo $37$ is the integer $0\\le x<37$ such that\n\n$$\nx+13≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,37).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that when we add our two numbers, we get a number that is *not* congruent to zero modulo $37:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14+13 & ≡27 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,37) \\\\  & ≢0 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,37)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude the following:\n\nThe integer $14$ $\\text{is not}$ the additive inverse of $13$ modulo $27$ since $14+13≢0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,37).$",
